export const KNOWLEDGE = "# Rick Hollandsworth — portfolio site content (rickhollandsworth.com)\n\nEverything below is published on Rick's site. Text written in the first person (\"I\", \"my\") is Rick speaking on his own site.\n\n## Home page — https://rickhollandsworth.com/\n\nPrincipal / Staff Product Designer · 15+ years\n\n### Trading floors to family wrists.\n\nI design high-stakes products for the people who depend on them: FX trading screens at EBS BrokerTec, including EBS Direct FX Forwards at $41B a day, and Verizon's connected-family apps and wearables, where every product serves two people at once. Most recently I led a 30-person design organization across Verizon's consumer portfolio.\n\nSee the work\nGet in touch\n\n20M Subscribers reached Verizon consumer portfolio\n\n30 Person design org led Verizon Family · 5 product squads\n\n$41B Daily volume, FX Forwards EBS Direct trading screen\n\n5× Best Broker, Spot FX EBS BrokerTec, consecutive years\n\nShipped for\n(image: Verizon)\n(image: EBS BrokerTec)\n(image: JPMorgan Chase Private Bank)\n(image: Citigroup)\n(image: BNY Mellon)\n(image: KPMG)\n\n### Selected work\n\nFive case studies\n\nEarlier work\nSelected engagements before Verizon and EBS. Details on request.\n\n- JPMorgan Chase Private Bank Unified legacy banker tools into one single sign-on workstation\n\n- Citigroup Prime Finance Portal architecture and integration into Citi Velocity\n\n- BNY Mellon / Pershing AVP, Multimedia Design; led a 10-person team on flagship brokerage platforms\n\n- EBS BrokerTec Watch EBS mobile market data, validated with traders\n\n### About\nSpringfield, MO\n\n(image: Rick Hollandsworth)\n\nI'm a hands-on Principal Product Designer with more than 15 years in telecom and financial services, and I've led teams when the work called for it. My work sits where mistakes are expensive: a trader acting on a price, or a parent trusting a watch on their child's wrist.\n\nAt Verizon I managed a 30-person cross-functional design organization across five product squads to consolidate and launch the unified Verizon Family app. After launch, as a new Director took over the team, I moved back into a hands-on Principal role. My work spanned a consumer portfolio that reached more than 20 million subscribers, including 5G Home Internet, Verizon Cloud, Verizon Family, Verizon Protect and the Gizmo and Care Smart wearables. Before that I spent five years at EBS BrokerTec designing FX trading interfaces, and earlier designed for JPMorgan Chase Private Bank, Citigroup, KPMG and BNY Mellon.\n\nI lead by giving designers ownership of hard problems, pushing for testing with real users, and getting design into the room before requirements are set. Today I use AI tools (Claude, Gemini, NotebookLM, Figma Make) for research synthesis and fast interactive prototyping.\n\n### Focus\n\nComplex financial interfaces, multi-user consumer ecosystems, design systems, design leadership\n\n### Education\n\nPratt Institute, BFA (Honors), Computer Graphics & Interactivity\n\n### Certifications\n\nClaude 101 (Anthropic, 2026) · Claude Design Fundamentals (SkillsBooster Academy, 2026) · Google AI Professional Certificate · ACI Dealing Simulation\n\n### Recommendations\nFrom managers, peers and direct reports\n\n\"Rick Hollandsworth is a valuable leader on the Family Design team, demonstrating commitment and strong engagement with the unified Verizon Family app throughout its development. He champions the customer's needs and upholds solution quality, ensuring that quality benchmarks are consistently met and aligned with design standards. Throughout the year, Rick has strengthened relationships with Product and Technology partners, fostering greater collaboration and better processes. He's adapted to evolve our sprint design process with earlier design involvement to help establish requirements and set up more frequent touchpoints to better support our designers. He effectively manages the often dynamic and at times high-pressure situations with tight deadlines, and shifting priorities, driving impactful solutions while effectively negotiating project constraints and stakeholder expectations. He's supported the overall success of the broader Product Design team, as he has played a key role in recruiting, hiring, and onboarding new members, and helping establishing a strong and cohesive team.\"\n\n— Paul Lafata Director of Family Products Verizon · Manager · Performance review, Dec 2024\n\n\"Rick excels at providing guidance while fostering independence. During a recent challenging project involving feature accuracy due to shifts in user technology, Rick supported me as I proposed solutions, identified touchpoints, and worked through dependencies. Rather than being prescriptive, he encouraged me to take ownership of the problem while offering thoughtful support. This approach enabled explorations that framed the problem and offered solutions holistically.\"\n\n\"Rick's support for exploration and iteration, especially with complex projects, is one of his greatest strengths. He creates space for designers to take ownership of problems and develop diverse, innovative solutions, empowering the team with motivation and confidence in their work.\"\n\n— Melissa Clarke Verizon · Direct report · Performance review, Dec 2024\n\n\"Rick continues to be an important leader for the +Protect portfolio. He has taken ownership and accountability to ensure business expectations are met from a delivery standpoint, while also ensuring that his team gets support in form of guidance, feedback and mentorship.\"\n\n\"This past year, Rick has made great progress in building trust and deepening the relationships with key stakeholders and partners. He helped establish better ways of collaborating across multiple groups and opening up communications. Rick and his team have made a crucial contribution around Individual Profiles, Brand 3.0 expansion, and work around the Security Dashboard as a foundation for a future +Protect Dashboard.\"\n\n\"In 2022 Rick took on the mentorship of a designer outside of his team and he has helped them to grow and progress in their career. Rick continues to be a key partner in hiring new talent as well as getting them up-to-speed on product as well as organizational intricacies.\"\n\n— Horst Frankenberger Sr. Manager - Experience Design Verizon · Manager · Performance review, Dec 2022\n\n\"Rick was an awesome teammate to work with on the 5G Home project, one of Verizon's top initiatives in 2018 to being the first to launch 5G. He brought his A game to every meeting and went above and beyond his role to ensure the success of the project. As a colleague who was senior to me, Rick always made me feel like an equal when we collaborated and gave me meaningful career advice. Rick is truly a defender of users. He has a deep understanding of the business and pushes for solutions that puts users first.\"\n\n— Avegail Tizon (https://www.linkedin.com/in/avegailtizon/) Pharmacy & Healthcare UX Verizon · Same team · LinkedIn, Mar 2019\n\nShow 22 more recommendations Show fewer\n\n\"Rick has always been an advocate for more user testing. This is a critical part of the design process and has always proven useful when incorporated into the Family project. The team has had great success when testing new features and the design has always improved after receiving user feedback.\"\n\n— Chris Ciafardini Verizon · Agency designer on Rick's team · Performance review, Dec 2024\n\n\"Throughout 2019 Rick has taken on more of a leading role within the design team as well as cross functional teams. He is well respected for his collaborative and thorough work style, his knowledge, and thoughtfulness. He is always on top of things and frequently suggests improvements proactively. Rick has helped business partners to better understand and adopt a user-centered, data-driven product development approach. Rick also helped to develop and socialize a more effective and efficient product development process to improve collaboration and efficiency. He managed through challenging times when the team was drastically understaffed by taking on more responsibilities. He was always willing to jump in and help where needed.\"\n\n— Horst Frankenberger Sr. Manager - Experience Design Verizon · Manager · Performance review, Dec 2019\n\n\"Rick is a reliable and participating team member. He relentlessly negotiates with our IT partners to provide the best experience. He always has his eye on the customer. As a CX team member, he is very dedicated to the best customer experience. His familiarity with the equipment and networks enabled him to evaluate all outcomes. He also looked at competition to arrive at the best course for VZW.\"\n\n\"He was readily available to his cross-functional team. Whenever anyone on the team had questions, he was there to answer them. He delivered designs across all digital platforms and communicated them in a constructive way that was easily understandable to align with the requirements/user stories being delivered to IT. He stays on top of his deliveries. Also, the format & file structure for final designs uploaded to Zeplin simplified the process & made it easy to locate specific screens.\"\n\n— John R. Devanney Director of Experience Design, 5G & New Consumer Products Verizon · Manager · Performance review, Dec 2018\n\n\"From day 1 Rick stepped in and blended in very nicely with the culture of our team. He has been a huge asset since he started. In little time, it became clear that Rick is fearless with taking on new projects. He excels at taking on a chaotic and daunting task… organizing it and making it happen.\"\n\n\"Rick took it up on himself to spread his knowledge of the Axure system to the entire team. Rick was not asked to do this.. he took it upon himself to improve our team's process. Rick held 2 sessions that were open to the entire team to train them on tips and tricks in the program. This showed Rick's dedication to better the team and use the tools to their full capacity to make our work easier and more streamlined.\"\n\n\"A project that deserves specific recognition is Rick's dedication to developing a 'best-in-class' Customer Journey for the upcoming 5G Initial / Full Commercial Launch. This initial /full launch is so valuable to the future of Verizon. Rick worked very hard in little time to take an existing 4G Home Fusion Customer Journey document and modify for our 5G initial / full rollout. This required Rick to come up to speed quickly on both products and work over the 2017 holiday season to meet the aggressive deliverable date.\"\n\n— Brian McGee Sr. Manager - Experience Design Verizon · Manager · Performance review, Dec 2017\n\n\"I worked with Rick at EBS BrokerTec for approximately 4 years. Rick has always impressed me with his level of professionalism. He is always looking to improve upon his own knowledge by educating himself with the most recent trends in his field. With his various knowledge and creativity he constructs intuitive clear designs. He is relentless in creating the proper interface for the end user, he will ask many pertinent questions and ensure end users are providing valuable input. Rick has been a key contributor into EBS BrokerTec new front end which is highly intuitive and on the forefront of trading interfaces. He is a key contributor to the success of new products. He will be a key contributor to the success of any organization in need of a highly skilled user interface expert.\"\n\n— Douglas Nevistich (https://www.linkedin.com/in/douglas-nevistich-76b5652/) Senior Director Product Manager - FX Quote Driven Market at CME Group EBS · Same team · LinkedIn, May 2017\n\n\"Rick is a talented and creative UX resource. He develops and challenges ideas and is always passionate about his work. Rick is an asset in every team and works extremely well collaborating both with business and technology personnel as required. I have no hesitation in recommending Rick.\"\n\n— Nichola Hunter (https://www.linkedin.com/in/nichola-hunter-918ab2a/) Head of EBS Product Management EBS · Manager · LinkedIn, Apr 2017\n\n\"I have had the pleasure of working with Rick on several major initiatives. His creativity and innovative spirit is matched by his professionalism and dedication to get projects completed. He has the ability to work closely with the business heads to translate their requirements into a workable blueprint for the development leads to implement. One of Rick's best attributes is attention to detail, even while working on an aggressive timetable for delivery. His work ethic and experience would make an excellent asset to any organization.\"\n\n— Peter Capuano (https://www.linkedin.com/in/peter-capuano-0898964/) Head of Product Delivery at NEX EBS · Different team · LinkedIn, Mar 2017\n\n\"I have seen some of Rick's outstanding design work at EBS. He did a fantastic job on several strategic, time challenged product design efforts and some of them involved new, ground breaking experience in foreign exchange trading systems. He has excellent skills and knowledge in user interfaces, prototype designing for various forms of application delivery including high performing web apps. I have seen his work first hand, top notch!! He strives for the best.\"\n\n— Srinivas K (https://www.linkedin.com/in/svkalidindi/) Product Leader | Consumer & Data Platforms · Digital Commerce · Supply Chain Citi · Different team · LinkedIn, Aug 2015\n\n\"Rick reported into me on a portal strategy project for a large financial services firm. During this engagement, he was required to stretch beyond his normal site design role, to one in which he needed to develop and communicate a set of usability recommendations to a business audience. I was quite impressed by Rick's ability to grow into this broader consultative role, and his willingness to seek out, internalize and apply constructive feedback. This is indicative of Rick's strong desire to continuously learn and grow professionally.\"\n\n— Scott Leavitt (https://www.linkedin.com/in/scottleavitt/) Retired on a lake when not exploring wild places @ the world… LDS · Manager · LinkedIn, Jan 2010\n\n\"I worked with Rick at iNautix/Pershing/Bank of New York Mellon over several years. We have worked together on several projects, including the integration of Apple and Windows networks. Rick is a very enthusiastic technologist and it shows in his work. He is a team player and his positive attitude made him a welcomed member on any project team. He was also a well respected member of the design team for his technical and organizational skills. It is my pleasure to recommend Rick.\"\n\n— Andy Surujnarine (https://www.linkedin.com/in/surujnarine/) Strategic IT Leader | AI, Cloud & Cybersecurity | Turning Emerging Tech Into Business Value BNY Mellon · Different team · LinkedIn, Jul 2009\n\n\"Rick was a key user experience contributor and valued collaborator on our project team. He successfully balanced innovation and UX best practices while meeting the requirements of the client. Rick is just an all around great team member.\"\n\n— Charlie Monica (https://www.linkedin.com/in/charliemonica/) Principal Consultant at Self Employ LDS · Same team · LinkedIn, Mar 2009\n\n\"I had the pleasure of working with Rick at LDS. He is a very detail oriented person strict to notice on details conforming to the UX best practices, a huge requirement for his profession. He is amiable and easy to work with. I have seen him work on a project with due diligence working beyond what is required of him making sure that the specs of the project and the deadline are met ahead of time. I definitely recommend him. He will be a valuable asset to any corporate organization.\"\n\n— Leo Primero, PhD (https://www.linkedin.com/in/leoprimero/) UX/UI Design Specialist & Educator LDS · Same team · LinkedIn, Mar 2009\n\n\"While I only worked with Rick for a short while, it was evident that he has a unique combination of deep-rooted technical, analytical and usability/user experience design skills that I, as a project manager, do not often see in a single project team member.\"\n\n\"Rick can readily analyze business requirements, interpret user experience requirements, overlay respective best practices and subsequently recommend a design and determine its techincal feasibility. His usability experience is also advantageous in that he, alone, can plan and conduct user-based design validations (one-on-one, focus group, etc.), and subsequently recommend remediation solutions.\"\n\n\"Rick also has a warm, friendly personality that clients and colleagues will feel comfortable with in a working environment.\"\n\n— Joseph Flesch (https://www.linkedin.com/in/josephflesch/) Advisor, difference-maker and do-er for companies and their leaders and teams LDS · Same team · LinkedIn, Mar 2009\n\n\"Rick is a detail-oriented user experience expert, but he does not lose sight of the big picture where business needs are concerned. He knows his experience method and when to apply them. I know his collaborative approach to design—involving clients in the design process—won him the respect of many a client. He's a first-rate professional, who takes his work seriously, but also prizes the interpersonal relationships that help get the work done. I'd be thrilled to work with him again.\"\n\n— Christopher Korintus (https://www.linkedin.com/in/christopher-korintus/) Design research powering digital transformation. LDS · Same team · LinkedIn, Mar 2009\n\n\"I had the pleasure of working with Rick on a SharePoint project where Rick did a significant part of the user experience design. It was a tough job because he replaced someone who had bounced off the project without notice and Rick had to pick up where the other designer had left off. I was amazed at the speed at which Rick came up to speed and the fluency with which he blended his own ideas with the current flow of the project. Rick is a very talented guy. On top of that, Rick is a total professional. I saw him work with some very difficult people and always with a smile and in a very professional manner. It would be a pleasure to work with Rick again and were I in a position to hire a User Experience Senior level or team lead, Rick would be at the top of my list.\"\n\n— Michael Bierly (https://www.linkedin.com/in/michael-bierly-1a61834/) Health insurance for individuals and small groups. LDS · Different team · LinkedIn, Mar 2009\n\n\"Rick is a talented, dependable and highly professional graphic designer and web developer. As Skyworks' first Art Director, his work was instrumental in building relationships with many early Fortune 500 clients. I would not hesitate to hire Rick again. He's a class act.\"\n\n— Garry Kitchen (https://www.linkedin.com/in/garrykitchen/) President/CEO and Co-founder Skyworks Interactive, Inc. · Senior colleague · LinkedIn, May 2008\n\n\"I've worked with Rick Hollandsworth for many years at Pershing. I found him to be an exceptionally talented and driven co-worker.\"\n\n\"I would strongly recommend Rick to any organization. He is an honest worker and would be a great asset to anyone's team.\"\n\n— Marcus Iorio (https://www.linkedin.com/in/marcus-iorio-9a72724/) VP Pershing LLC BNY Mellon · Different team · LinkedIn, May 2008\n\n\"This memo is to recommend Rick Hollandsworth as an employee to your organization. I was the Vice President of Training for the Pershing Technology Group and have worked with Rick on Multimedia projects. Rick assisted the training department with the many elearning initiatives. He is very talented and creative which helped us to deliver the media to the clients quickly and accurately. He was great to work with and met his deadlines timely.\"\n\n\"Rick is a \"self-starter\" and is highly motivated to achieve his goals. I am always impressed with Rick's integrity, responsibility and dependability. He has a positive attitude and always displays a generous level of excitement in all endeavors, even under the most difficult or stressful situations.\"\n\n\"Rick has strong inter-personal skills in developing business relationships with internal IT groups, Vendors and Business Managers. He is a team player and can bring energy to the group to get the most difficult tasks done.\"\n\n\"Please accept this note as the highest recommendation for Rick. He will be an asset to your department and organization.\"\n\n— Colleen M. Connell (https://www.linkedin.com/in/colleenconnell/) Digital Literacy Coordinator at the Plainfield Public Library BNY Mellon · Different team · LinkedIn, Apr 2008\n\n\"Rick and I work in partnership to cater to businesses with UI needs. He is a very talented individual and a very good team-mate. He has set the bar high for perfection and always shoots for the best UI solution. He uses his previous experience in the UI domain in a perfect situation. He has an abundance of innovative ideas to handle various situations. He has been very helpful and positive. We have been a successful partnership in terms of work distribution, problem solving, and discussing design solutions and taking decisions on UI standards. Besides, I do want to mention that he is a very good human being, kind and warm hearted.\"\n\n— Shilpa Patange (https://www.linkedin.com/in/shilpapatange/) Design Evangelist | Design Thinking Champion | Innovator in User-Centered Solutions JP Morgan · Different team · LinkedIn, Apr 2008\n\n\"I have worked with Rick Hollandsworth over 6 years at Pershing. He is very well organized, produces genuine creativeness in his designs, consistently demonstrates the ability to remain focused, and maintains utmost professionalism under tightest deadlines. He is a true assets to any group that he joins.\"\n\n— Vladimir Winecoor (https://www.linkedin.com/in/vnwinecoor/) Senior VP UX | UI Designer / Motion Designer BNY Mellon · Same team · LinkedIn, Mar 2008\n\n\"I worked with Rick on many projects over the years. Rick worked as a visual and multimedia designer. I found that Rick's abilities extend to many aspects of multimedia design, including visual elements, digital sythensis, transformations and blending of different media, etc. Rick helped produce training materials, web site designs, and also helped with overall branding strategy. It's been great working with him.\"\n\n— Bijan Marjan (https://www.linkedin.com/in/bijanmarjan/) Senior Technical Project Manager BNY Mellon · Different team · LinkedIn, Mar 2008\n\n\"Rick worked on our User Experience Team within the Business Strategy division and I had the pleasure of working with Rick for 6 months. During this time, he was a dependable team member who contributed innovative ideas and used sound design practices.\"\n\n\"He was always cheerful and willing to assist with any task. In addition, Rick was motivated and interested in learning.\"\n\n\"I would not hesitate to recommend Rick as a User Experience Professional.\"\n\n— Elizabeth Benko Holmes (https://www.linkedin.com/in/elizabethholmes/) Director User Experience Strategy JP Morgan · Same team · LinkedIn, Mar 2008\n\nMore on LinkedIn (https://www.linkedin.com/in/rickhollandsworth/)\n\n### Let's talk.\n\nOpen to Staff, Principal, Lead and Director product design roles, and to consulting engagements. Available to travel for meetings and events as needed.\n\nEmail: rick.hollandsworth@outlook.com (mailto:rick.hollandsworth@outlook.com)\n\nPhone: +1 (201) 404-5031\n\nLinkedIn: rickhollandsworth (https://www.linkedin.com/in/rickhollandsworth/)\n\nRésumé: Download PDF (https://rickhollandsworth.com/assets/pdf/rick-hollandsworth-resume.pdf)\n\n## Case study: Verizon Family — https://rickhollandsworth.com/work/verizon-family/\nPage title: Verizon Family app redesign · UX case study\nSummary: How Rick Hollandsworth led a 30-person design organization to consolidate five Verizon family apps into one, turning NPS around by nearly 20 points in a year.\n\n01 · Verizon\n\n### Verizon Family\n\nConsolidating five legacy family products into one app, and turning NPS around by nearly 20 points in a year.\n\n(image: Verizon Family app screens: profiles, safe walk alerts, live map, family line calling and real-time tracking)\n\nProfiles · Safe walk · Live map · Family calling\n\nMy role: Led a 30-person cross-functional design organization across five autonomous product squads: 8 full-time designers, 8 contractors and 14 agency partners from Publicis Sapient.\n\nScale: 950K+ paying subscriptions; about 1.2M users migrating from five legacy products\n\nTimeline: 3-month redesign sprint; unified app launched H2 2024\n\n### The problem\n\nVerizon's family products were spread across five separate apps: Smart Family, GizmoHub, Care Smart, Hum and Roadside. Each carried years of accumulated settings, nothing gave the household a shared picture, and third-party vendor latency slowed everything down. Sentiment showed it: combined NPS sat at −17.\n\n### Key decisions\n\n- Cut legacy features instead of porting them. Product managers and squad leads pushed hard for 1:1 feature parity to keep power users happy. I held the line on strict deprecation and backed it with rapid qualitative testing, which showed leadership that legacy setting clutter was the main source of user confusion and negative reviews. We sunset the low-value controls and simplified the core information architecture.\n\n- Organize around family profiles, not devices. Location, place alerts, safety check-ins, group communication and driving insights all hang off a person, so the app still makes sense when a child changes phones or gets a watch.\n\n- Set feature depth by age and role. Each capability surfaces at the depth that fits the member: tight controls for a young child, lighter check-ins and driving insights for a teen.\n\n### Outcome\n\n+19 pts NPS swing in 12 months, from −17 to +2\n\n4.4–4.8★ App store ratings\n\n−2.47% Net churn\n\nMeasured launch to launch over 12 months, combined NPS rose from −17 to +2, reaching +7 on iOS. Simpler onboarding and a cleaner daily experience drove the recovery, and new activations outpaced cancellations, so net churn turned negative across 950K+ subscriptions.\n\nFull case study (PDF) (https://rickhollandsworth.com/assets/pdf/verizon-family-case-study.pdf)\n\nPassword required. It's in my application materials, or ask me for it by email (mailto:rick.hollandsworth@outlook.com?subject=Case%20study%20password%20request) or LinkedIn message (https://www.linkedin.com/in/rickhollandsworth/).\n\n## Case study: Verizon Gizmo Watch — https://rickhollandsworth.com/work/gizmo-watch/\nPage title: Verizon Gizmo Watch kids' smartwatch · UX case study\nSummary: Designing a smartwatch worn by a six-year-old and trusted by a parent: on-watch UX, the GizmoHub companion app and award-winning child-safety design.\n\n02 · Verizon\n\n### Verizon Gizmo Watch\n\nA smartwatch worn by a six-year-old and bought, set up and trusted by a parent.\n\n(image: Gizmo Watch screens: AI assistant, weather, alerts, video calling and texting, water lock and to-do lists)\n\nAsk & Learn · Weather · Alerts · Video calling · Water lock · To-do lists\n\nMy role: Lead / Principal Experience Designer across Gizmo Watch 2, 3 and 4, with my deepest involvement on Gizmo Watch 4: end-to-end UX/UI architecture, hardware-software interaction, on-watch OS navigation and the GizmoHub companion app. Also authored the UX analysis and product roadmap.\n\nScale: 700K+ active parent accounts managing 2M+ connected family contacts\n\n### The problem\n\nThe watch is worn by a child but bought, configured and trusted by a parent. Every new feature had to be simple enough for a six-year-old's wrist and clear enough to earn a parent's confidence from the companion app.\n\n### Key decisions\n\n- Design every feature as a pair. Each feature (Ask & Learn AI assistant, weather, alerts, video calling and texting, water lock, to-do lists) has an on-watch interaction and a GizmoHub counterpart for the parent, designed together.\n\n- Keep the wrist simple and put control with the parent. The watch shows a child only what they need in the moment. Setup, rules and trusted contacts (up to 10–20 per watch, including multiple guardians) live in GizmoHub.\n\n- Design for a child who grows. The feature set extends the watch's useful life as children age into it, instead of being outgrown in a year.\n\n### Outcome\n\nForbes Best Kids Smartwatch Overall, Gizmo Watch 4 (2026)\n\n2019 GOOD DESIGN Award, GizmoWatch\n\n2023 Red Dot Award, Gizmo Watch 3\n\nForbes Vetted (https://www.forbes.com/sites/forbes-personal-shopper/article/best-kids-smartwatch/) named the Gizmo Watch 4 its Best Kids Smartwatch Overall in 2026 after hands-on family testing. The ecosystem serves 700K+ active parent accounts managing more than 2M connected family contacts. The GizmoWatch won a GOOD DESIGN Award in 2019, and Gizmo Watch 3 and Gizmo Watch 3 Adventure won a Red Dot Award in 2023 for child-oriented UX/UI design and integrated safety features.\n\nFull case study (PDF) (https://rickhollandsworth.com/assets/pdf/verizon-gizmo-watch-case-study.pdf)\n\nPassword required. It's in my application materials, or ask me for it by email (mailto:rick.hollandsworth@outlook.com?subject=Case%20study%20password%20request) or LinkedIn message (https://www.linkedin.com/in/rickhollandsworth/).\n\n## Case study: Verizon Care Smart — https://rickhollandsworth.com/work/care-smart/\nPage title: Verizon Care Smart senior smartwatch · UX case study\nSummary: A smartwatch and family app that give older adults independence and their families reassurance: radically simple watch UX, an intentional Get Help slider and fall detection.\n\n03 · Verizon\n\n### Verizon Care Smart\n\nIndependence for an older adult and reassurance for their family, without making either feel monitored.\n\n(image: Care Smart on Apple Watch: Get Help, Trust Circle, Profile and Test call)\n\nOn the wrist · Get Help · Trust Circle\n\nMy role: Principal Experience Designer for the watch experience and the family companion app.\n\nAudience: Older adults living independently (the wearer), and the adult children and family who act as caregivers\n\nTimeline: Launched March 2020; expanded to Apple Watch with 24/7 professional monitoring and fall detection (2021–22) and Samsung Galaxy Watch4+ on Wear OS (Q2 2023)\n\n### The problem\n\nAn older adult wants independence, not supervision. Their adult child wants reassurance without intruding. One product had to serve both people without making either feel watched, and an emergency feature had to be fast without being easy to trigger by mistake.\n\n### Key decisions\n\n- Keep the watch radically simple. High-contrast text, simplified circular touch targets, a small fixed set of trusted contacts and plain language.\n\n- Make Get Help intentional. Testing with older adults showed frequent false emergency dispatches from accidental taps on the prominent emergency button. We replaced the single tap with a slider, which eliminated accidental dispatches without slowing access in a real emergency.\n\n- Move the complexity to the family app. Configuration, medication reminders, check-in status and remote Medical ID management live with the caregiver, along with low-friction location alerts and fall notifications.\n\n- Frame safety as a companion, not a monitor. Fall detection, location and the Trust Circle are presented as support the wearer chooses, with group chat keeping the family connected day to day.\n\n### How we validated it\n\nWe tested with both sides of the dual-user model: senior wearers, focusing on contrast, touch targets and accidental-trigger prevention, and family caregivers, who needed fast location alerts, fall notifications and remote Medical ID management.\n\n(image: Care Smart companion app: location dashboard)\n\n(image: Care Smart companion app: fall detected, 911 engaged)\n\n(image: Care Smart companion app: Trust Circle)\n\n(image: Care Smart companion app: group chat)\n\nFamily app · Location · Fall alert · Trust Circle · Group chat\n\n### Outcome\n\n0 Accidental emergency dispatches after the slider redesign\n\n3 Watch platforms: Care Smart, Apple Watch, Galaxy Watch\n\n24/7 Professional monitoring with fall detection\n\nCare Smart grew from a dedicated senior smartwatch into a service across Apple and Samsung wearables, extending Verizon's wearable portfolio to a new life stage.\n\nFull case study (PDF) (https://rickhollandsworth.com/assets/pdf/verizon-care-smart-case-study.pdf)\n\nPassword required. It's in my application materials, or ask me for it by email (mailto:rick.hollandsworth@outlook.com?subject=Case%20study%20password%20request) or LinkedIn message (https://www.linkedin.com/in/rickhollandsworth/).\n\n## Case study: One screen for spot and forwards — https://rickhollandsworth.com/work/ebs/\nPage title: EBS BrokerTec FX trading platform · UX case study\nSummary: One HTML5 trading screen for EBS Market spot and EBS Direct FX Forwards, validated with traders in London and New York: $41B daily volume, 10,000 institutional users.\n\n04 · EBS BrokerTec (now CME Group)\n\n### One screen for spot and forwards\n\nAn HTML5 trading screen that brought EBS Market spot and the new EBS Direct FX Forwards into one place, validated on the trading floor.\n\n(image: Redesigned HTML5 trading screen with blue EBS Market spot price cards and green EBS Direct bilateral price cards)\n\nAfter · EBS Market spot (blue) and EBS Direct forwards (green) on one screen\n\nMy role: Senior User Experience Designer, 2012–2017. Embedded with trading product and platform engineering; designed the new HTML5 trading front end.\n\nScale: $41B average daily volume in EBS Direct FX Forwards, alongside EBS Market spot at $100B+ a day\n\n### The problem\n\nEBS was expanding beyond its central limit order book for spot into bilateral liquidity with EBS Direct, a new platform for FX Forwards (outrights and currency swaps). Forwards still ran largely on phone and chat, and traders were already split across a native Windows C++ app, an Adobe Flex app and early HTML: three interaction models for one high-stakes task repeated thousands of times a day.\n\n### Key decisions\n\n- Put both markets on one screen. Blue price cards carry EBS Market spot; green cards carry EBS Direct bilateral liquidity. Traders read both sources side by side without switching applications.\n\n- Lay out the screen by the trader's scan path. High-density pricing grids, depth ladders and order entry are placed for peripheral reading, so a trader can watch several pairs while acting on one.\n\n- Redesign iceberg orders for speed. Traders slice, hide and execute large-block spot orders through high-density tiles and hotkeys instead of a multi-step modal. This workflow drove a 30% acceleration in Spot FX on $100B average trading volume.\n\n- Turn multi-leg tickets into one action. Forward and swap quotes that took a phone call or chat thread and a multi-field ticket became single-click responses and custom hotkeys.\n\n- Build the safety rails into the ticket. Automated pre-trade credit checks, pre-populated deal templates and strict tolerance bounds replaced manual entry, removing the source of fat-finger errors on multi-million-dollar notionals.\n\n### How we validated it\n\nOn-floor usability sessions with active institutional bank traders, FX desk heads and liquidity providers in London and New York. We tested the pricing grids, depth ladders and hotkey execution flows against split-second trading under heavy volatility.\n\n(image: Legacy native Windows C++ trading application)\n\nBefore · Windows C++\n\n(image: Legacy Adobe Flex trading application)\n\nBefore · Adobe Flex\n\n### Outcome\n\n$41B ADV, EBS Direct FX Forwards\n\n10,000 Active institutional users\n\n30% Spot FX acceleration from the iceberg redesign\n\nThe platform quickly reached 10,000 active institutional users across tier-1 market-making banks and liquidity providers. Single-click quotes and hotkeys replaced phone and chat negotiation, automated credit checks removed manual ticket errors, and EBS won \"Best Broker for Spot FX\" five years in a row.\n\nFull case study (PDF) (https://rickhollandsworth.com/assets/pdf/ebs-case-study.pdf)\n\n## Case study: FinFront UI SDK — https://rickhollandsworth.com/work/finfront/\nPage title: FinFront UI SDK fintech design system · Case study\nSummary: A 75-component fintech design system across five financial verticals, with live documentation and working prototypes, built Claude-first by Rick Hollandsworth.\n\n05 · Self-directed, 2026\n\n### FinFront UI SDK\n\nOne designer, five financial verticals, 75 components, and no room for drift between the docs and the product. Built Claude-first.\n\nMy role: Sole designer and product lead: research, IA, visual language, tokens, component behavior and documentation\n\nScope: 75 components · 5 verticals (FX/Prop, RIA, CRE & Alts, Commodities, Core) · 17 user roles · 3 device targets\n\nNote: A self-directed prototype for portfolio demonstration, not a licensed product. All data is synthetic.\n\n### The problem\n\nA trading terminal, an advisor workspace and a GP capital-raise desk all need to agree on color, numbers and what a severity flag means, without becoming the same app in five costumes. Meanwhile, trading platforms keep rebuilding the same order books and blotters, and private markets (cap rates, target IRR, GP/LP waterfalls) have almost no designed patterns at all.\n\n### Key decisions\n\n- Make the library the source of truth, not a record of it. Every token, class and component is extracted from the five working prototypes' production CSS, so documentation can't drift from what it documents. Tokens follow a three-tier model (primitive, semantic, component), generated from JSON.\n\n- Share foundations, scope the components. All verticals inherit one token layer, but each scopes its own component classes under a platform wrapper, so RIA's severity chip never collides with CRE's. A sixth vertical follows the rule instead of inventing a new one.\n\n- Keep two color systems apart on purpose. One severity ladder (critical to low) runs across Prop Desk, RIA and Commodities. CRE & Alts gets its own capital-lifecycle colors (uncalled, invested, distributed), because forcing it onto the severity ladder would make two unrelated ideas compete for the same color. I logged that as a decision rather than quietly overloading a token.\n\n- Document live, not static. Every component in the library is interactive. Switching theme, density or colorblind-safe mode restyles every demo across all five verticals at once, because the library runs on the same CSS custom properties as the prototypes.\n\n### How I worked with AI\n\nI directed the research synthesis, personas and IA, visual language and tokens, and component behavior and interaction rules. Claude drafted fintech requirements docs, React / TypeScript and CSS scaffolding, and generated and QA'd the library sections, which let five verticals iterate in days instead of months. Every deliverable went through a headless render-and-check pass before it shipped.\n\nWhere I overrode it. The first render of the FX trading screen looked plausible but didn't hold up to a trader's eye: visual elements were misaligned, the column-customization controls in the upper right didn't work, and the RFQ buttons did nothing. The root problem was that Claude treated RFQ as one generic flow. I walked it through how the request-for-quote workflow differs by asset type, had it rebuild against those workflows, and re-checked every control before moving on. AI got me to a first draft fast; domain knowledge decided what was actually correct.\n\n(image: FinFront SDK product lineup across desktop, tablet and mobile trading screens)\n\nOne system across desktop, tablet and mobile\n\nDesign library · Light and dark themes from one token set\n\n### Outcome\n\n75 Live, documented components\n\n5 Working sector prototypes\n\n17 User roles designed for\n\nA headless React 19 / TypeScript library and design system with live demos, code, props and behavior notes, plus five working sector prototypes built on one token foundation.\n\nOverview (PDF) (https://rickhollandsworth.com/assets/pdf/finfront-overview.pdf)\nDesign library (PDF) (https://rickhollandsworth.com/assets/pdf/finfront-design-library-case-study.pdf)\nAI assistant (PDF) (https://rickhollandsworth.com/assets/pdf/finfront-ai-assistant-case-study.pdf)\n\nLive component library walkthrough available in interviews.\n\n## Downloadable PDFs\n- https://rickhollandsworth.com/assets/pdf/rick-hollandsworth-resume.pdf\n- https://rickhollandsworth.com/assets/pdf/ebs-case-study.pdf\n- https://rickhollandsworth.com/assets/pdf/finfront-overview.pdf\n- https://rickhollandsworth.com/assets/pdf/finfront-design-library-case-study.pdf\n- https://rickhollandsworth.com/assets/pdf/finfront-ai-assistant-case-study.pdf\n- https://rickhollandsworth.com/finfront/ (short link to the FinFront overview PDF)\n- https://rickhollandsworth.com/ai/ (short link to the FinFront AI Assistant case study PDF)\n\n## Résumé\nRick Hollandsworth — Résumé (plain text, maintained by hand from assets/pdf/rick-hollandsworth-resume.pdf)\nHeadline: Principal Product Designer | User-Centered Design | AI-assisted design\nLocation: Springfield, Missouri\n\nSummary\nPrincipal Product Designer with 15+ years of expertise driving end-to-end user journeys and high-level UX strategy for a $1.25 billion consumer product portfolio reaching 20 million subscribers. A proven leader proficient in user-centric design and rigorous Agile execution, partnering with executive leadership and cross-functional teams to bridge complex user needs with core business goals.\n\nKey career achievements\n- Consumer product scale (Verizon): directed UX strategy for a $1.25B consumer portfolio (including 5G Home Internet, Cloud, Family, Protect, Gizmo), reaching over 20M subscribers.\n- FinTech innovation (EBS BrokerTec): architected award-winning trading interfaces that processed $100B+ in daily trades and accelerated Spot FX volume by 30%.\n- Asset and revenue growth (Bank of New York / Pershing): led design for flagship brokerage platforms generating $330M annually and managing $30B in assets.\n- Global consolidation (JPMorgan Chase): unified legacy systems into a turnkey single sign-on platform for bankers managing $25M+ accounts, elevating global rankings.\n\nCareer timeline (oldest first)\n1. Bank of New York – Pershing LLC, Assistant Vice President, Multimedia Design (1998–2007)\n2. JPMorgan Chase Private Bank, Senior User Interface Designer and Information Architect (2007–2008)\n3. UI/UX consulting for KPMG, Citigroup and Medco, Senior Design Consultant (2008–2012)\n4. EBS BrokerTec (now CME Group), Senior User Experience Designer (2012–2017)\n5. Verizon, UX Design Lead → Lead Experience Designer → Principal Experience Designer (2017–2026)\n\nExperience (most recent first)\n\nVerizon — July 2017 to August 2026 — Basking Ridge, NJ\nTitles: Principal Experience Designer (May 2021 – Aug 2026); Lead Experience Designer (Oct 2018 – May 2021); UX Design Lead (Jul 2017 – Oct 2018)\n- Team leadership and scaling: hired and managed a design organization of nearly 30 designers and managers (full-time employees, contractors and agency partners) across the Verizon product ecosystem.\n- Managed high-impact portfolios: influenced design strategy for Verizon Cloud ($1B revenue, 5.74M users) and 5G Home Internet ($514M revenue).\n- Specialized solutions: delivered user-centric ecosystems for the Gizmo Watch (about 700K active kids' watches and 2.4M contacts) and the Verizon Family suite ($220M revenue).\n- Security engagement: drove adoption for Verizon Protect ($17.7M revenue) and integrated security dashboards to build user trust.\n- AI-driven design: uses AI tools (Gemini, NotebookLM, Figma Make) for user research, market research and data analysis, design concepts and rapid interactive prototyping.\n\nEBS BrokerTec (now CME Group) — March 2012 to March 2017 — Parsippany, NJ\nTitle: Senior User Experience Designer\n- Designed a new FX Forwards trading interface processing $41B in daily trades; the platform won Best Broker for Spot FX five consecutive years.\n- Supported a 30% acceleration in Spot FX trading through iceberg order designs on a platform averaging $100B in daily volume.\n\nUI/UX Consulting (KPMG, Citigroup, Medco) — May 2008 to February 2012\nTitle: Senior Design Consultant\n- KPMG: produced intranet templates that enforced Section 508 accessibility requirements for the entire firm.\n- Citigroup: supported Prime Finance business development by integrating legacy products into the Citi Velocity application within strict branding guidelines.\n- Medco Health Solutions: designed a new B2B homepage and interactive communication center, from sitemaps to final UI specifications.\n\nJPMorgan Chase Private Bank — May 2007 to May 2008 — New York, NY\nTitle: Senior User Interface Designer and Information Architect\n- Consolidated legacy systems into a turnkey single sign-on experience for bankers managing $25M+ minimum accounts, contributing to the firm's ranking as the #1 Private Bank Globally by Euromoney.\n\nBank of New York – Pershing LLC — September 1998 to April 2007 — Jersey City, NJ\nTitle: Assistant Vice President, Multimedia Design\n- Directed a 10-person design team through major acquisitions, leading product design for flagship brokerage applications that generated $330M in annual revenue and managed $30B in customer assets.\n- Products won multiple CODiE Awards and a #1 overall ranking from Gomez Advisors for Best Online Brokerage Service.\n\nCore competencies\n- Design and strategy: envisioning and maintaining end-to-end customer journeys; articulating design decisions to key stakeholders.\n- Methodologies: Design Thinking, User-Centered Design, user task and workflow analysis, end-user research and analysis.\n- Process leadership: leading design processes, overseeing team capacity, managing project master files alongside local design systems.\n- Execution: information architecture, wireframes and interactive prototypes, usability testing.\n\nEducation and certifications\n- Pratt Institute, BFA (Honors), Computer Graphics and Interactivity, New York, NY\n- Anthropic Claude 101 certification (2026)\n- Google AI Professional Certificate and Google AI for App Building (2026)\n- ACI Dealing Simulation certification\n\nTools and skills\nFigma, Figma Make, Sketch, Adobe suite, Adobe Firefly, UserTesting.com, Google Gemini, NotebookLM, Claude, Claude Design. Visual and interaction design, human-centered design, wireframes and prototypes, web, mobile and watch apps, information architecture, user and market research, usability testing, iOS and Android design.\n\n## Additional background (approved by Rick)\n\n## How Rick describes his focus\n- Specialties: enterprise design systems and multi-device UX leadership across consumer telecommunications, IoT connected wearables, and institutional fintech/SaaS platforms.\n- Design philosophy, which Rick calls \"Speaking Human\": digital products should be sophisticated on the back end but effortless on the front end. He grounds decisions in user research, quantitative metrics and cross-functional empathy across Product, Engineering and Design.\n\n## Skills\n- UX strategy and leadership: end-to-end UX strategy and portfolio architecture; multi-squad leadership, mentoring and hiring (a 30-person organization across 5 squads at Verizon); customer journey mapping; user task and workflow analysis; information architecture; alignment with Product Management, Technical Product Development (TPD) and executive leadership.\n- Design-to-engineering handoff: \"zero-drift\" handoffs using Gherkin-style acceptance criteria, Agile sprints and daily design critiques.\n- Design systems: enterprise design systems and token architecture, including Verizon Design System (VDS) 3.0 adoption for Verizon Family and the FinFront UI SDK (75 documented components across desktop, mobile and smartwatch targets).\n- Multi-device UI: iOS and Android, desktop web and native, smartwatch (watchOS, Wear OS and custom watch operating systems), and tablet.\n- Data-dense interfaces: high-density pricing grids and complex data visualization.\n- Accessibility: Section 508 and WCAG AA.\n- AI-assisted workflows: Google Gemini, Gemini Canvas, NotebookLM, Claude and Figma Make for research synthesis, generating component tokens, code-assisted app simulations and rapid prototyping.\n- Tools: Figma, Figma Make, Axure RP, Sketch, Adobe Creative Suite, UserTesting.com.\n\n## Verizon — more detail on the consumer portfolio\nRick directed UX strategy for a $1.25B Verizon consumer portfolio reaching 20M+ subscribers.\n- Verizon Family / Smart Family ($220M revenue): consolidated fragmented parent and child apps into one Verizon Family experience; redesigned location tracking, parental controls, driving insights and trusted contacts; addressed friction in pairing and permissions. Results: combined NPS rose from −17 to +2 within a year (+7 on iOS), net churn turned negative (−2.47%), app store ratings reached 4.4–4.8 stars, and subscriptions passed 950K (about 1.2M users).\n- Gizmo Watch and GizmoHub: smartwatch for children aged about 6–11 with safe communication and no open internet. Rick designed on-watch touch interactions, circular navigation and the GizmoHub parent app, across Gizmo Watch 2, Gizmo Watch 3 and Gizmo Watch 3 Adventure. Scale: about 700K active kids' watches and 2.4M contacts. Awards: GOOD DESIGN Award (2019) and Red Dot Design Award (2023).\n- Care Smart: older adults were triggering emergency alerts by accident. Rick's team tested directly with older adults and family caregivers and replaced the single-tap emergency trigger with an intentional slider, cutting accidental alerts without slowing down real emergencies. The platform later expanded to Apple Watch and Samsung Galaxy Watch4+.\n- 5G Home Internet ($514M revenue, 5.7M users): channel-neutral customer journeys for 5G Home Internet and 5G Home Voice, including mesh router onboarding and out-of-box setup.\n- Verizon Cloud ($1B revenue, 5.74M users): unified product roadmaps across mobile and desktop to simplify backup and onboarding.\n- Verizon Protect, Digital Secure and the security dashboard ($17.7M revenue for Verizon Protect): integrated conversational support through the Security Advisor chatbot, aligned with Verizon Assistant.\n- Design systems: Rick led adoption of Verizon Design System (VDS) 3.0 for Verizon Family.\n\n## EBS BrokerTec (now CME Group) — more detail\n- EBS Direct served 10,000+ institutional bank traders, with usability work in London and New York.\n- Iceberg orders: traders slice, hide and execute large-block spot orders through high-density tiles and keyboard hotkeys.\n- Errors: manual ticket entry was replaced with single-click quote responses, and pre-trade credit checks were built into the order ticket, removing the source of multi-million-dollar \"fat-finger\" errors.\n\n## Earlier career — more detail\n- JPMorgan Chase Private Bank (2007–2008): the single sign-on workstation served private bankers managing high-net-worth accounts with $25M+ minimums.\n- BNY Mellon / Pershing (1998–2007, Assistant Vice President, Multimedia Design): led 10 designers through a series of broker-dealer acquisitions (PCFN, DLJdirect, CSFBdirect, Harrisdirect). The brokerage platforms generated $330M in annual revenue and managed $30B in customer assets, and won multiple CODiE Awards (including Best Online Brokerage Service and Best Product) and a #1 overall ranking from Gomez Advisors.\n\n## SaaS experience (B2C and B2B)\nWhen visitors ask about SaaS, subscription products, cloud platforms, B2B or enterprise software, this is Rick's relevant experience. He has 15+ years leading end-to-end UX and product strategy across consumer (B2C) and business (B2B) software-as-a-service.\n- B2C consumer SaaS and subscription products at Verizon:\n  - Verizon Cloud ($1B a year in recurring revenue, 5.7M+ active users): multi-tier consumer cloud storage subscription (500GB, 2TB and Unlimited plans) across iOS, Android, macOS and Windows.\n  - Verizon Family / Smart Family ($220M a year in recurring revenue, 950K+ subscriptions, about 1.2M users): subscription family safety, location and parental-control app suite.\n  - Digital Secure and Mobile / Home Protect: security, identity protection and device support subscription platform.\n- B2B and enterprise SaaS platforms:\n  - EBS BrokerTec / CME Group: Senior User Experience Designer for EBS Direct, a B2B FX trading platform used by 10,000+ institutional bank traders, with $41B in daily FX Forwards volume.\n  - BNY Mellon Pershing: enterprise brokerage web platforms, including NetX360, for wealth managers and financial institutions ($30B in customer assets).\n  - JPMorgan Chase Private Bank: enterprise single sign-on workstation for private bankers.\n  - KPMG and Medco Health Solutions: enterprise B2B web portals and firm-wide intranet software.\n  - FinFront UI SDK: headless React UI component infrastructure for fintech and trading platforms (self-directed).\n  - Habita IQ: a self-directed B2B fintech subscription index prototype, designed across three subscription tiers (Essentials, Professional and Institutional).\n\n## What Rick is looking for\n- Staff, Principal, Lead and Director product design roles, in enterprise SaaS, fintech, CX platforms and consumer ecosystems.\n- Also open to consulting engagements.\n- Contact: rick.hollandsworth@outlook.com, LinkedIn https://www.linkedin.com/in/rickhollandsworth/, or the \"Get in touch with Rick\" button.\n";
export const PAGES = {"home": {"title": "Home — Rick Hollandsworth, Principal / Staff Product Designer", "url": "https://rickhollandsworth.com/"}, "verizon-family": {"title": "Verizon Family app redesign · UX case study", "url": "https://rickhollandsworth.com/work/verizon-family/"}, "gizmo-watch": {"title": "Verizon Gizmo Watch kids' smartwatch · UX case study", "url": "https://rickhollandsworth.com/work/gizmo-watch/"}, "care-smart": {"title": "Verizon Care Smart senior smartwatch · UX case study", "url": "https://rickhollandsworth.com/work/care-smart/"}, "ebs": {"title": "EBS BrokerTec FX trading platform · UX case study", "url": "https://rickhollandsworth.com/work/ebs/"}, "finfront": {"title": "FinFront UI SDK fintech design system · Case study", "url": "https://rickhollandsworth.com/work/finfront/"}};
